  "full_response": "Git handles branching and merging using a fundamentally different approach compared to systems like Subversion (SVN). Here’s a detailed explanation of how Git manages these operations internally:\n\n### Branching\n\n1. **Lightweight and Cheap**:\n   - In Git, a branch is simply a lightweight movable pointer to a commit. By default, every Git repository has a single branch called `master` (or `main` in newer conventions), though you can create any number of branches.\n   - Creating a branch is incredibly fast because it only involves creating a new pointer for the branch, not duplicating the entire project's data.\n\n2. **Commit IDs**:\n   - Each commit in Git is identified by a SHA-1 hash. Branches only hold the commit ID of their latest commit.\n\n3. **HEAD**:\n   - The `HEAD` is a reference to the current branch you are on. When you checkout a new branch, `HEAD` points to that branch, allowing you to easily switch between branches.\n\n### Merging\n\n1. **Fast-Forward Merge**:\n   - If the branch you are merging has not diverged from the branch you are merging into (i.e., your branch is a descendant of the branch you are merging), Git performs a \"fast-forward\" merge. It simply moves the `HEAD` of the current branch to point to the latest commit of the branch being merged.\n\n2. **Three-Way Merge**:\n   - If the branches have diverged, Git uses a three-way merge strategy:\n     - **Base**: The common ancestor of the two branches.\n     - **A**: The tip of the current branch (the one you are merging into).\n     - **B**: The tip of the branch being merged.\n   - Git uses these three commits to automatically merge the changes. A new merge commit is typically created, with the merged changes from both branches.\n\n3. **Merge Commits**:\n   - When branches are merged, if there have been changes in both branches since they diverged, a new commit known as the \"merge commit\" is created. This commit has two parents: one from each branch being merged.\n\n4. **Conflict Resolution**:\n   - If conflicting changes are detected during a merge, Git cannot automatically resolve them. You must manually intervene to resolve the conflicts. Git marks the conflicting areas in the files, and you need to edit these conflicts and then commit the merge.\n\n5. **Rebasing as an Alternative**:\n   - Before merging, you can also",
